body:
When a library is blocked, the code forces an error state by attempting to `dlopen()` a hard-coded nonexistent path. This ensures `dlerror()` is non-NULL, but the resulting error message will reference `/anylinux_blocked_lib_that_does_not_exist.so` instead of the actually blocked library, which can make debugging harder for callers that log `dlerror()`. Consider making the intentionally-missing filename incorporate the requested library name (e.g., include the basename) so `dlerror()` is still non-NULL but more actionable.
```suggestion
		// Use a fake path that incorporates the basename of the blocked library so
		// the resulting dlerror() message is more informative.
		const char *base = filename;
		const char *slash = strrchr(filename, '/');
		if (slash && slash[1] != '\0') {
			base = slash + 1;
		}
		char fake_path[512];
		snprintf(fake_path, sizeof(fake_path),
			 "/anylinux_blocked_%s_does_not_exist.so", base);
		(void)dlopen_orig(fake_path, RTLD_NOW);
```

